  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $Y∼U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a)=0,F(b)=1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a<x<b.$ Then, the random variable $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX={F}^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y=F(x).$ To do this, we solve the following equation for $x,$ taking into account that $0\\le y\\le 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}) \\\\ \\frac{y\\pi }{2} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{y\\pi }{2}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{y\\pi }{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y=0.321,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{0.321\\pi }{2}))≈-0.595$\n\n- For $y=0.567,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{0.567\\pi }{2}))≈0.212$\n\n- For $y=0.804,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{0.804\\pi }{2}))≈1.146$",
